TITLE:: ParaEQ
summary:: a five band parametric eq, as a UGen and as an object that drives it
categories:: Libraries>ParaEQ
related:: Classes/FxUnit, Classes/FxChain, Classes/ParaEQGUI, Classes/ParaEQPanel, Classes/ParaEQView, Classes/ParaEQSpectrum, Classes/ModuleEQ, Classes/BLowShelf, Classes/BPeakEQ, Classes/BHiShelf

DESCRIPTION::
Five biquads in series - low shelf, three peaks, high shelf - each band a
code::[freq, dB, q]:: triple. The filter is one pseudo-UGen, link::#*ar::, so it
runs wherever a UGen does; and an instance of this class drives it wherever it
runs: a control on an Ndef or a Synth, a node of its own on a bus, or a place in
an link::Classes/FxChain::.

code::
s.boot;
Ndef(\pad, { var sig = Saw.ar([110, 110.7]) * 0.1; ParaEQ.ar(sig, \eq) }).play;

e = ParaEQ(Ndef(\pad), \eq);      // drives that Ndef's \eq control
e.gui;
e.preset = \telephone;
e.setBand(0, 80, -9, 0.8);        // low shelf, 80 Hz, -9 dB, rs 0.8
::

The same object with a node of its own, in place on a bus:

code::
e = ParaEQ.onBus(0, 2);
e.enabled = true;
::

Everything about how it is driven - targets, buses, chains, what
code::enabled:: does in each case - is link::Classes/FxUnit::'s and documented
there. This file is about the eq.

subsection:: Bands

A band is code::[freq, dB, q]::, numbered 0 to 4. code::q:: is an code::rs:: on
the two shelves and an code::rq:: on the three peaks - smaller is narrower - and
the floor differs accordingly: 0.6 for a shelf, 0.05 for a peak. Gain is clipped
to ± link::#*dbRange::, frequency to link::#*minFreq:: .. link::#*maxFreq::.
Inside those limits the filters stay well behaved.

Changes reach the node immediately and are lagged there by link::#*lag::
seconds, so dragging a handle is a slide rather than a step.

subsection:: The curve is the filter

link::#-responseAt:: works the dB curve out from the same coefficients the
server runs - link::#*coeffs:: is the BEQSuite arithmetic in numbers - so the
window draws the filter you hear, not a sketch of it. That is the one thing this
class has to get right: change link::#*ar:: and link::#*coeffs:: together.

subsection:: Presets and the clipboard

Every eq shares one preset list - see link::#*presetNames:: - and one clipboard,
so a curve saved on an Ndef's eq is in the menu of a OneShot pad's, and a curve
copied on a pad pastes onto the master chain's. Your own presets are kept in
link::#*presetFile::, in the support folder rather than the repository.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar
The filter, as a UGen.

ARGUMENT:: in
The signal, any number of channels.

ARGUMENT:: name
The control the bands arrive on: fifteen numbers, code::[freq, q, dB]:: per band,
made with link::Classes/NamedControl::. A second control, code::name:: with
code::On:: appended, crossfades the whole thing to dry.

ARGUMENT:: bypass
code::false:: leaves the code::On:: control out, for a node that is freed rather
than switched - what link::#*synthDef:: and ModuleCommon's slot def do.

ARGUMENT:: lag
Seconds the bands are lagged by; link::#*lag:: if left out.

RETURNS:: The filtered signal, through link::Classes/Sanitize::.

METHOD:: new
Drive a control on a node. See link::Classes/FxUnit#*new::.

ARGUMENT:: target
An Ndef, a Synth, a Group - anything that answers to code::set:: - or nil for an
eq something else pushes.

ARGUMENT:: controlName
code::\eq:: by default.

METHOD:: onBus
A node of its own, in place on a bus. See link::Classes/FxUnit#*onBus::.

METHOD:: defName
METHOD:: synthDef
The in place synth for a channel count: code::In:: - link::#*ar:: - code::XOut::
under an envelope, so switching in and out leaves the dry signal rather than
silence.

METHOD:: numBands
5.

METHOD:: bandNames
code::["low shelf", "peak 1", "peak 2", "peak 3", "high shelf"]::.

METHOD:: bandName
The name of one band.

METHOD:: bandTypes
code::\lowShelf::, code::\peak:: ×3, code::\hiShelf::.

METHOD:: isShelf
Whether a band index is one of the two shelves.

METHOD:: qLo
METHOD:: qHi
The width limits for a band: 0.6 or 0.05 at the bottom, 10 at the top.

METHOD:: defaultBands
A fresh copy of the flat setting.

METHOD:: dbRange
How far a band may be pushed either way, and the range the curve is drawn over.
24 by default.

METHOD:: minFreq
METHOD:: maxFreq
20 and 20000 by default.

METHOD:: lag
How quickly the filters follow a change, in seconds. 0.1 by default.

METHOD:: fade
How long a node of its own takes to fade in and out when it is switched. 0.02.

METHOD:: presetNames
Every preset, the ones the library ships first: code::\flat::, code::'rumble
cut'::, code::\warm::, code::\bright::, code::\distant::, code::\loudness::,
code::\telephone::, then yours.

METHOD:: presets
METHOD:: userPresets
METHOD:: allPresets
The same as code::name, bands, name, bands...::.

METHOD:: presetBands
A copy of one preset's bands, or nil.

METHOD:: isUserPreset
Whether a name is yours, and so can be written over or dropped.

METHOD:: addPreset
Keep a curve under a name. It joins the menu of every window and is written to
link::#*presetFile::. A name the library ships is refused, and so is a curve that
is not five bands long. Answers the name, or nil.

code::
ParaEQ.addPreset(\scooped,
    [[80, 3, 0.8], [250, 0, 1], [900, -8, 1.2], [3500, 0, 1], [8000, 4, 0.8]]);
::

METHOD:: removePreset
Drop one of yours.

METHOD:: clearUserPresets
Drop all of yours, on disk as well.

METHOD:: presetFile
Where your presets are kept - code::paraeq-presets.scd:: in
link::Classes/Platform#*userAppSupportDir::. Settable. ModuleEQ's older
code::module-eq-presets.scd:: is read once if this one does not exist yet.

METHOD:: presetStore
The link::Classes/FxPresetStore:: behind all of the above.

METHOD:: clipboard
The curve link::#-copyBands:: last put aside, or nil.

METHOD:: hasClipboard
Whether there is anything to paste.

METHOD:: coeffs
One biquad as code::[a0, a1, a2, b1, b2]:: - the shape BEQSuite hands the
server, in numbers.

ARGUMENT:: type
code::\lowShelf::, code::\peak:: or code::\hiShelf::.
ARGUMENT:: sr
ARGUMENT:: freq
ARGUMENT:: q
ARGUMENT:: db

METHOD:: magnitudes
code::|H(e^jw)|:: of one set of coefficients at every frequency in an Array.

METHOD:: flatControls
The node's default: fifteen numbers, flat.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: bands
The five code::[freq, dB, q]:: triples. Setting takes a list of them and pushes
all five at once.

METHOD:: setBand
Move one band. nil leaves that part of it where it was.

ARGUMENT:: i
0..4.
ARGUMENT:: freq
ARGUMENT:: db
ARGUMENT:: q

METHOD:: freqAt
METHOD:: dbAt
METHOD:: qAt
One part of one band, with a two argument setter each: code::dbAt_(4, 3)::.

METHOD:: flat
Every band back to flat.

METHOD:: preset
Set the bands from a preset, by name.

METHOD:: presetName
The preset these bands are, or nil once you have moved one.

METHOD:: saveAsPreset
Keep this curve under a name - link::#*addPreset::.

METHOD:: copyBands
METHOD:: pasteBands
The shared clipboard. Only the curve moves, not whether the eq is on.

METHOD:: copyFrom
The same straight from another eq.

METHOD:: responseAt
The dB curve of every band over an Array of frequencies. Sum them for the curve
you hear.

METHOD:: controls
What the node is given: fifteen numbers, code::[freq, q, dB]:: per band.

METHOD:: settings
The bands, for a preset or the clipboard - link::Classes/FxUnit#-settings::.

METHOD:: asDict
METHOD:: fromDict
code::(enabled:, bands:):: - the shape ModuleCommon's kits and sets have always
written.

METHOD:: sampleRate
The server's, when it is up - what the curve is worked out at.

METHOD:: enabled
METHOD:: gui
METHOD:: panel
METHOD:: target
METHOD:: chain
METHOD:: synth
See link::Classes/FxUnit::.

EXAMPLES::

code::
s.boot;

// in a graph
Ndef(\pad, { var sig = Saw.ar([110, 110.7]) * 0.1; ParaEQ.ar(sig, \eq) }).play;
e = ParaEQ(Ndef(\pad), \eq);
e.gui;
e.preset = \loudness;
e.setBand(0, 60, -18, 0.8);       // lose the rumble
e.dbAt_(4, 4);                    // put some air back
e.enabled = false;                // dry, crossfaded
e.enabled = true;

// kept, and carried
e.saveAsPreset("my room");
e.copyBands;

// on the main out, in place
m = ParaEQ.onBus(0, 2);
m.enabled = true;
m.pasteBands;
m.gui;

m.free;
e.free;
Ndef(\pad).clear;
ParaEQ.removePreset("my room");
::
